## Claim
Text: "The U.S. surgeon general and the Centers for Disease Control both previously said that ‘masks are not effective in preventing [the] general public from catching coronavirus.’"
Claim author: Yinon Weiss
Claim date: October 29, 2020
Claim origin: https://thefederalist.com/2020/10/29/these-12-graphs-show-mask-mandates-do-nothing-to-stop-covid/

## Initial Q&A
### Did the U.S. Surgeon General (Dr. Jerome Adams) state in early 2020 that masks are not effective for the general public to prevent catching coronavirus?
Answer: Yes, U.S. Surgeon General Dr. Jerome Adams stated in early 2020 that masks are not effective for the general public to prevent catching coronavirus. For example, in a March 2, 2020 interview with "Fox & Friends," he said, "It has not been proven to be effective in preventing the spread of coronavirus amongst the general public" and added that "wearing a mask improperly can actually increase your risk of getting disease."

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/surgeon-general-against-masks/

### Did the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) issue guidance in early 2020 stating that masks are not effective for preventing the general public from catching coronavirus?
Answer: Yes, the CDC initially discouraged mask use for the general public and issued guidance indicating that masks were not effective for preventing the general public from catching the coronavirus. A CDC spokesperson stated that “CDC does not recommend that people who are well wear a facemask to protect themselves from respiratory illnesses, including COVID-19” and explained that “surgical masks won’t stop the wearer from inhaling small airborne particles,” implying they were not effective for that purpose ([Vox article](https://www.vox.com/2020/3/31/21198132/coronavirus-covid-face-masks-n95-respirator-ppe-shortage)).

Source URL: https://www.vox.com/2020/3/31/21198132/coronavirus-covid-face-masks-n95-respirator-ppe-shortage

### What was the exact wording of any statement by the U.S. Surgeon General regarding mask effectiveness for the general public before October 29, 2020?
Answer: In an interview on "Fox & Friends" on March 2, 2020, U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams stated: "One of the things [the general public] shouldn't be doing is going out and buying masks ... It has not been proven to be effective in preventing the spread of coronavirus amongst the general public ..."

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/surgeon-general-against-masks/

### What was the exact wording of any statement by the CDC regarding mask effectiveness for the general public before October 29, 2020?
Answer: The search result does not provide a direct quotation from the CDC itself, but rather a third-party letter quoting a CDC-published review article. The review from *Emerging Infectious Diseases* states: "We did not find evidence that surgical-type face masks are effective in reducing laboratory-confirmed influenza transmission, either when worn by infected persons (source control) or by persons in the general community to reduce their susceptibility." This statement refers to influenza, not SARS-CoV-2, and is based on pre-pandemic data; it is not an official CDC policy statement regarding masks for the general public against COVID-19. Therefore, the exact wording of a direct CDC statement on mask effectiveness for the general public before October 29, 2020, is not provided in this source.

Source URL: https://le.utah.gov/publicweb/BRISCJK/PublicWeb/43178/43178.html

### On what specific dates did the U.S. Surgeon General and the CDC make statements about masks not being effective for the general public?
Answer: According to the Snopes article, U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams made statements recommending against mask use for the general public on **February and March 2020**, with specific interviews on **March 2, 2020** (where he said “It has not been proven to be effective in preventing the spread of coronavirus amongst the general public”) and **March 31, 2020** (where he said “the data doesn’t show that wearing masks in public will help people”). For the CDC, the article notes that “the CDC… originally recommended against the general public wearing masks” but does not provide a specific date for that initial statement; the agency’s guidance changed on **April 3, 2020**, when the CDC recommended cloth face coverings. Therefore, the only specific dates given for the Surgeon General’s statements are March 2 and March 31, 2020, while the CDC’s earlier position is referenced without a precise date.

Source URL: https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/surgeon-general-against-masks/

### Were those statements made in the context of preserving medical-grade masks for healthcare workers during a shortage?
Answer: Yes, the statements were made in the context of preserving medical-grade masks for healthcare workers during a shortage. The article notes that the Surgeon General urged people to stop buying masks, stating they are not effective for the general public, and explicitly adds that "if there is a shortage of the masks, health care providers who do need them can't get them for sick patients."

Source URL: https://www.kcrg.com/content/news/US-Surgeon-General-face-masks-are-not-effective-in-preventing-spread-of-COVID-19-568391361.html

### Did the CDC later change its guidance on mask use for the general public after the initial statements?
Answer: Yes, the CDC later changed its guidance. According to a Time article, "On April 3, President Donald Trump announced that the CDC is changing its guidance to include a recommendation that all Americans wear non-medical masks, or fabric that covers a person’s face and nose, when they leave their house." This reversed earlier statements that masks were not effective for the general public.

Source URL: https://time.com/5774521/coronavirus-face-mask-prevention/

### Did the U.S. Surgeon General later revise or retract his earlier statement about mask effectiveness for the general public?
Answer: Yes, U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams later revised his earlier statement. In a CBS "Face the Nation" interview, he confirmed that the administration was "trying to correct" the earlier guidance against masks and urged the public to wear face coverings, explaining that "the science at the time suggested that there was not a high degree of asymptomatic spread. We learned more." (CBS News)

Source URL: https://www.cbsnews.com/news/coronavirus-surgeon-general-jerome-adams-wearing-masks-face-the-nation/

### Is there any evidence that the U.S. Surgeon General and the CDC both explicitly said masks are not effective in preventing the general public from catching coronavirus, as opposed to preventing transmission from infected individuals?
Answer: The search result provides direct evidence that U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams explicitly stated on Twitter that masks are "NOT effective in preventing general public from catching coronavirus," but it does not contain any equivalent statement from the CDC itself—only that Adams later asked the CDC to investigate whether the recommendation should change. Therefore, while the Surgeon General's remark is confirmed, there is no evidence in this result that the CDC explicitly said the same.

Source URL: https://www.cnbc.com/2020/04/02/coronavirus-us-surgeon-general-asks-cdc-to-see-if-face-masks-are-effective.html

### Did the statements refer to a specific type of mask (e.g., N95 respirators, surgical masks, or cloth masks) when claiming they are not effective for the general public?
Answer: Thesearch result does not specify a particular type of mask; it refers generally to "face masks" without distinguishing between N95 respirators, surgical masks, or cloth masks.

Source URL: https://www.kcrg.com/content/news/US-Surgeon-General-face-masks-are-not-effective-in-preventing-spread-of-COVID-19-568391361.html

## Final Judgement
**Step 1:** The evidence confirms that U.S. Surgeon General Jerome Adams explicitly stated in early 2020 that masks are not effective for preventing the general public from catching coronavirus, and the CDC initially issued guidance discouraging mask use for the general public, with a spokesperson stating that masks are not recommended for well people to protect themselves. Both statements were made before October 29, 2020. Although the CDC later changed its guidance and the Surgeon General revised his stance, these later developments do not undermine the fact that they previously made such statements. No evidence contradicts the Claim.

**Step 2:** The evidence is clear and sufficient; no further investigation is required.

**Step 3:** The claim that the U.S. surgeon general and the CDC both previously said that masks are not effective in preventing the general public from catching coronavirus is factually accurate based on the documented evidence. Therefore, the decision is `supported`.

### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
The U.S. Surgeon General Dr. Jerome Adams stated in a March 2, 2020 interview that masks are "not effective in preventing the spread of coronavirus amongst the general public" ([Snopes](https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/surgeon-general-against-masks/)). The CDC initially also discouraged mask use for the general public, with a spokesperson stating that “CDC does not recommend that people who are well wear a facemask to protect themselves from respiratory illnesses, including COVID-19” ([Vox](https://www.vox.com/2020/3/31/21198132/coronavirus-covid-face-masks-n95-respirator-ppe-shortage)). Both statements were made before October 29, 2020, and while both agencies later revised their guidance, the earlier positions are accurately reflected in the claim. Therefore, the claim is **supported**.